                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "73fd5677-45e7-3b86-a86b-3f6fe01a46ed",
                        "entry_id": "2ebdf873-9186-377e-9541-07b7f7ba4e6b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To lap or wrap around (someone or something); to envelop, to surround."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e14b8506-2213-4eab-b07b-20e2fe7ccad3",
                                "sense_id": "73fd5677-45e7-3b86-a86b-3f6fe01a46ed",
                                "text": "There was redy in a wode / Two hundurde knyghtys, þat were gode. / Owte of the wode þey came anon / And belapped vs euerychon.",
                                "translation": "There were ready in a wood / Two hundred knights, that were good. / Out of the wood they came anon / And surrounded us everyone.",
                                "english": "There were ready in a wood / Two hundred knights, that were good. / Out of the wood they came anon / And surrounded us everyone."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d5fd55eb-00fc-467c-bfd7-5064f34263e6",
                                "sense_id": "73fd5677-45e7-3b86-a86b-3f6fe01a46ed",
                                "text": "For the lytell vertue & strength of that nature the which remayneth / there is as who sayth a lytell sparke of fyre wrapped and hyd in asshes / This is the naturall reason of man belapped with great darkenes / yet hauing dyscrecion of good & yll / of truthe & falsenesse, though it be vnable to fulfyll all that he approueth, nor may nat vse yet the full lyghte of truth / nor his affeccyons helthfully"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a664fecb-2aa9-467c-8475-9b86f2d2baea",
                                "sense_id": "73fd5677-45e7-3b86-a86b-3f6fe01a46ed",
                                "text": "The wofull pule, with wattir wnluflly, / Withhaldis thaim so at thai may nocht go by; / And Stix, the flude, bylappis thaim about / Nyne tymis, sa close at thai sall neuer wyn out.",
                                "translation": "The woeful pool, with water unlovely, / Withholds them so that they may not go by; / And Styx, the flood, belaps them about / Nine times, so close that they shall never win out.",
                                "english": "The woeful pool, with water unlovely, / Withholds them so that they may not go by; / And Styx, the flood, belaps them about / Nine times, so close that they shall never win out."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "339bb488-c93c-4f31-af0e-048006a8d2f6",
                                "sense_id": "73fd5677-45e7-3b86-a86b-3f6fe01a46ed",
                                "text": "Ouer this the foresayd laye / Reporte howe the pope may / A holy anker call / Out of the stony wall / And hym a bysshop make / It he on hym dare take / To kepe so harde a rule / To ryde vpon a mule / With golde all betrapped / In purple and paule be lapped"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "596beb7c-baa6-42dc-84eb-f96fea23dad2",
                                "sense_id": "73fd5677-45e7-3b86-a86b-3f6fe01a46ed",
                                "text": "[T]here be many things that pertaine to a Chriſtian man, and yet al thoſe things are conteined in this one thing, that is, loue: be lappeth vp al things in loue."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0115286a-f031-462e-9ee4-81318b9aedd2",
                                "sense_id": "73fd5677-45e7-3b86-a86b-3f6fe01a46ed",
                                "text": "And it may be, if thou knewest not which were perfect meekness, thou wouldst imagine, when thou hadst a little knowledge and feeling of this, that I call imperfect meekenss, that thou hadst almost gotten perfect meekness; and so wouldst deceive thyself, weening that thou wert full meek, when thou wert indeed all belapped in foul stinking pride."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "64df3821-fa52-45fb-b9c7-969d4883a54d",
                                "sense_id": "73fd5677-45e7-3b86-a86b-3f6fe01a46ed",
                                "text": "Following the traditions of my elders and much of the advice which is generally so freely offered to young mothers, I fashioned skirts with bands so wide and cumbersome, so much belapped and pinned, as to reduce the unfortunate babe to semblance of a small mummy."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
